475
00:37:50,120 --> 00:38:07,120
It doesn't matter that pictures are taken as still images. They are analyzed using machine learning, which makes the features on your car searchable. And it doesn't matter that only one picture is taken. It's the automated data analytics, which pairs pictures together that creates a timeline of your movements.

476
00:38:07,120 --> 00:38:25,120
That's what makes ALPRs and all of these things tracking technologies. The databases are not secure. Hackers have been able to pull millions of images out of flock cameras and other things, and they are not limited when there are over 60 searches a day by HPD and the data is shared with outside agencies.

477
00:38:25,120 --> 00:38:40,120
Claiming that crime is down by 33 percent because of the installed LPRs in 2019 is conflating causation and correlation. There is no evidence to suggest that crime fell because of the cameras, especially because crime nationally fell at the same time.

478
00:38:40,120 --> 00:38:55,120
And all randomized control studies, which I have for you here and are actually the way we measure causation, do not find these technologies make people safer. In fact, in real life, mass surveillance has led to false arrests and endangerments of people's lives.

479
00:38:55,120 --> 00:39:13,120
