487
00:40:17,120 --> 00:40:27,120
Privacy advocates have raised concerns that the technology that this company sells that enables mass surveillance constitutes mass violations of the Fourth Amendment.

488
00:40:27,120 --> 00:40:37,120
The 2018 Supreme Court case Carpenter v. United States has been pointed to as more recent legal evidence of this.

489
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:44,120
The court in that case determined that warrants were required to obtain cell site location information for the purpose of tracking people's movements.

490
00:40:44,120 --> 00:40:54,120
Companies like LexisNexis essentially circumvent these kinds of requirements by allowing government agencies to buy commercially available data rather than having to compel it by means of a warrant.

491
00:40:54,120 --> 00:41:11,120
More recently, a lawsuit, Ramirez v. LexisNexis Solutions, was brought against the company in 2024 by a group of immigrants rights advocates alleging that LexisNexis' businesses practices open them up to increase law enforcement attention in violation of their constitutional rights.

492
00:41:11,120 --> 00:41:19,120
It also alleges unjust enrichment since the company profits from the data it aggregates while the people it originated from are not compensated and see no benefit.

493
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:27,120
Unfortunately, that case was dismissed due to lack of standing and because the plaintiffs in that case didn't demonstrate actual damages.

494
00:41:27,120 --> 00:41:31,120
So these arguments haven't actually been tested in court.

495
00:41:31,120 --> 00:41:39,120
