Good evening, members of the City Council and Mayor Battle.

1910
02:40:59,120 --> 02:41:03,120
My name is Lisa Montgomery and I live at 124 Walker Avenue in Anseville.

1911
02:41:03,120 --> 02:41:23,120
I'm here tonight at the urging of my City Council member Bill Kling and on behalf of the growing coalition of families, working professionals and longtime residents who surround the Meridian Street corridor of the Quigley Arts and Entertainment District to include the Old Town Historic District.

1912
02:41:23,120 --> 02:41:29,120
I want to begin by reminding this body of the very foundation of Huntsville's noise ordinance.

1913
02:41:29,120 --> 02:41:44,120
It opens with a clear declaration of policy that every resident of the city deserves to live peacefully, protected from excessive disturbing noises that jeopardize their health, tranquility and the quiet enjoyment of their property.

1914
02:41:44,120 --> 02:41:55,120
I'm here tonight because the city's expansion of outdoor amplified entertainment into the Meridian Street extension of the Quigley District has stripped that fundamental right away from our neighbors.

1915
02:41:55,120 --> 02:41:57,120
This is not a new conflict.

1916
02:41:57,120 --> 02:42:10,120
The city's legal friction with this quarter stretches all the way back to a major municipal court trial on May 28, 2003, following a wave of environmental noise citations.

1917
02:42:10,120 --> 02:42:14,120
For 23 years, residents have begged for relief.

1918
02:42:14,120 --> 02:42:25,120
When this council amended the noise ordinance on December 2, 2021, with a narrow 3 to 2 vote, it was openly framed as a temporary experimental trial.

1919
02:42:25,120 --> 02:42:29,120
Over two decades of data have given us the definitive answer.

1920
02:42:29,120 --> 02:42:32,120
The experiment on Meridian Street has failed.

1921
02:42:32,120 --> 02:42:42,120
The city has already taken the primary venue in this corridor to court at least twice over noise violations, yet the disruptions are escalating.

1922
02:42:42,120 --> 02:42:51,120
For example, this Friday, tomorrow, the venue is hosting Hell's Bells, an ACDC tribute band on an outdoor patio.

1923
02:42:52,120 --> 02:42:59,120
The heavy low frequency bass and stadium level volume cannot be contained by a simple patio barrier.

1924
02:42:59,120 --> 02:43:05,120
Furthermore, the city's current enforcement tools are technically unequipped to handle it.

1925
02:43:05,120 --> 02:43:11,120
Standard decibel meters filter out low frequencies and completely fail to pick up bass.

1926
02:43:12,120 --> 02:43:21,120
A venue can read it as legally compliant on a city meter while physically rattling the windows of our homes.

1927
02:43:21,120 --> 02:43:34,120
To make matters worse, every Thursday night is biker night, bringing hours of excessively loud revving engines with motorcycles coming and going until 1.30 in the morning, well past any reasonable curfew.

1928
02:43:34,120 --> 02:43:39,120
Due to time constraints, I'm going to turn the second half of our presentation over to John Anderson.

1929
02:43:39,120 --> 02:43:42,120
Thank you for your time and your service to our city.

1930
02:43:45,120 --> 02:43:47,120
Thank you, Ms. Montgomery.

1931
